-- title:
--   Reduced localised modular Laurent series lie in k(̃ j(q),̃ j(q^N))
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a commutative ring, $A \subseteq L$ a subring, $k$ a field, $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$ a ring homomorphism, and $N$ a nonzero natural number. Let `modularRing N A` be the subring of the Laurent series ring $L((q))$ generated by the constant series attached to the elements of $A$ (the range of `constSeries A`) together with the two series `jqModC L`, the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}(1 + \cdots)$ of $j$ with integer coefficients pushed into $L$, and `jqNModC L N`, obtained from it by the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$. The subring `modularLocalized N A red` consists of those $f \in L((q))$ for which there are $r, s \in$ `modularRing N A` with $s$ outside the prime ideal `redKer` and $f \cdot s = r$; on it, `modularRedLocHom N A red` is the ring homomorphism to $k((q))$ given by `redLoc`, characterised by $\mathrm{redLoc}(f) \cdot \mathrm{redRes}(s) = \mathrm{redRes}(r)$, where `redRes` denotes the coefficientwise reduction along $\mathrm{red}$ of elements of the modular ring. The assertion is that for every $f \in$ `modularLocalized N A red`, with no further hypothesis, the image `modularRedLocHom N A red f` lies in `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, the intermediate field of $k((q))$ over $k$ generated by `jqModC k` and `jqNModC k N`.
--
--   This is the statement that reduction modulo the kernel of $\mathrm{red}$ carries the localised modular ring into the function field $k(\tilde j(q), \tilde j(q^N))$ of the level-$N$ modular curve over $k$ in its $q$-expansion incarnation; equivalently, `modularRedLocHom` corestricts to a ring homomorphism into that field. It is used throughout the construction of the characteristic-$p$ fibre model, for instance in identifying the series annihilated by the reduction map and in producing fibre models on the cuspidal chart.
